# GitHub PR attribution — no Cursor participation

Cloud Agent runs as the `cursor[bot]` GitHub account. Any push, force-push, title rename, or commit authored as **Cursor Agent** (`cursoragent@cursor.com`) makes Cursor appear on the PR as a participant or timeline actor. GitHub timeline events are permanent and cannot be deleted.

## Commits

- Author and committer must be the repo owner (`tonywanglab <tonywanglab@users.noreply.github.com>`), not Cursor Agent.
- Do **not** add `Co-authored-by: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>` trailers.
- Before pushing, verify with `git log -1 --format='author: %an <%ae>%ncommitter: %cn <%ce>'`.

## Pushes and PR updates

- **Never force-push** to fix authorship or history. Force-pushes from `cursor[bot]` create permanent timeline entries.
- Do **not** rename PR titles after creation — title changes appear as `cursor[bot]` activity.
- Avoid any git push from this environment when the goal is to remove Cursor from a PR.

## If Cursor already appears on a PR

1. Rewrite commit authorship locally (author + committer → repo owner; strip Cursor co-author trailers).
2. Create a **new branch** with a non-`cursor/` name via the GitHub refs API (pointing at the rewritten tip), not a force-push to the existing branch.
3. Open a **new PR** with the final title set at creation time.
4. Close the old PR. Timeline pollution on closed PRs cannot be removed; do not try to "clean up" the old one with more bot actions.

## PR bodies

- Prefer PR descriptions without Cursor agent footer links when updating is possible.
- Do not perform bot-driven PR edits (title, body, force-push) when the user asks to remove Cursor from PR visibility.
